{
  "case_id": "2025-24-38",
  "document_hash": "a4e0123e8dcad8f81811394cf804b00b777f08f484aaea3de6d500fa3743b4c4",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2025-24-38",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a4e0123e8dcad8f81811394cf804b00b777f08f484aaea3de6d500fa3743b4c4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a4e0123e8dca-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "uncertain",
      "text": "Idaho's law classifies on the basis of sex because sex is what matters in sports. It correlates strongly with countless athletic advantages, like size, muscle mass, bone mass, and heart and lung capacity. Tragically but not surprisingly, male athletes have even injured female athletes in many sports. If women don't have their own competitions, they won't be able to compete. Gender identity does not matter in sports, and that's why Idaho's law does not classify on the basis of gender identity. It treats all males equally and all females equally regardless of identity, and its purpose is exactly what the legislature said,"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2025-24-38",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a4e0123e8dcad8f81811394cf804b00b777f08f484aaea3de6d500fa3743b4c4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a4e0123e8dca-0000-0003",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: We will hear argument first this morning in Case 24-38, Little versus Hecox."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Hurst",
      "case_id": "2025-24-38",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a4e0123e8dcad8f81811394cf804b00b777f08f484aaea3de6d500fa3743b4c4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a4e0123e8dca-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "uncertain",
      "text": "It's not all different, Your Honor, and that's exactly what we're concerned about, that their arguments about needing to make exceptions from intermediate -- from an otherwise valid classification for people for whom that classification doesn't make sense, those arguments don't limit themselves to people who identify as transgender. Many males could say: I can't really compete with the women's basketball team, and, therefore, I should be able to try out."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Hurst",
      "case_id": "2025-24-38",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a4e0123e8dcad8f81811394cf804b00b777f08f484aaea3de6d500fa3743b4c4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a4e0123e8dca-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "uncertain",
      "text": "Our case involves the issue of the constitutional definition of sex, which the Ninth Circuit made a decision about, what sex means for constitutional purposes. The Fourth Circuit did not make that decision. We would be bound by that decision going forward unless the Court reaches that question."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2025-24-38",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a4e0123e8dcad8f81811394cf804b00b777f08f484aaea3de6d500fa3743b4c4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a4e0123e8dca-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "No, that wasn't the premise of the question. It was just an attempt to explore the consequences of deciding the mootness issue one way or the other."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Hurst",
      "case_id": "2025-24-38",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a4e0123e8dcad8f81811394cf804b00b777f08f484aaea3de6d500fa3743b4c4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a4e0123e8dca-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "uncertain",
      "text": "No, Your Honor, nothing changed externally to --to the plaintiff. The plaintiff's affidavit filed in this Court says that there has been negative attention and so forth since the beginning of this case. The only thing that changed is this Court granted certiorari. And then, after that, the plaintiff said: I want out and so I will stop playing sports."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2025-24-38",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a4e0123e8dcad8f81811394cf804b00b777f08f484aaea3de6d500fa3743b4c4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a4e0123e8dca-0001-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 29,
        "start": 29
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "You would say that we have to force an unwilling plaintiff who has offered to dismiss with prejudice, promised not to incur this activity again, we would force that person to continue prosecuting this case?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2025-24-38",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a4e0123e8dcad8f81811394cf804b00b777f08f484aaea3de6d500fa3743b4c4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a4e0123e8dca-0001-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 30,
        "start": 30
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Mr. Hurst, I want to take you back to our conversation about whether there's such a thing as as-applied equal protection challenges and ask you for two things."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2025-24-38",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a4e0123e8dcad8f81811394cf804b00b777f08f484aaea3de6d500fa3743b4c4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a4e0123e8dca-0001-0008",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Would your constitutional position be any different in that situation?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Barrett",
      "case_id": "2025-24-38",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a4e0123e8dcad8f81811394cf804b00b777f08f484aaea3de6d500fa3743b4c4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a4e0123e8dca-0001-0011",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Your friends on the other side say that, listen, science is uncertain and so we need more factual development, it's not really clear how much of an athletic advantage boys and men have if their testosterone levels are below a certain point. How does that play out? Tell me why we don't need more fact finding or what is the state's burden in -- in -- in showing what the state of scientific certainty is."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Hurst",
      "case_id": "2025-24-38",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a4e0123e8dcad8f81811394cf804b00b777f08f484aaea3de6d500fa3743b4c4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a4e0123e8dca-0002-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "uncertain",
      "text": "MR. HURST: So, first -- first, as to administrability, Your Honor, making sure that a -- a transgender athlete does not lack an -- or does not have an unfair advantage would require ongoing testosterone monitoring because their testosterone can fluctuate. That is invasive, that is intrusive, and that's expensive."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Jackson",
      "case_id": "2025-24-38",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a4e0123e8dcad8f81811394cf804b00b777f08f484aaea3de6d500fa3743b4c4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a4e0123e8dca-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 47,
        "start": 47
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE JACKSON: I -- I -- I understand that. And there are -- there are legal arguments. Let me ask you something about the classification. I guess I'm struggling to understand how you can say that this law doesn't classify on the basis of transgender status. The law expressly aims to ensure that transgender women can't play on women's sports teams. So why is that not a classification on the basis of transgender status?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States",
      "case_id": "2025-24-38",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a4e0123e8dcad8f81811394cf804b00b777f08f484aaea3de6d500fa3743b4c4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a4e0123e8dca-0002-0002",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 50,
        "start": 50
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "uncertain",
      "text": "It is undisputed that states may separate their sports teams based on sex in light of the real biological differences between males and females. States may equally apply that valid sex-based rule to biological males who self-identify as female. Denying a special accommodation to trans-identifying individuals does not discriminate on the basis of sex or gender identity or deny equal protection."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2025-24-38",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a4e0123e8dcad8f81811394cf804b00b777f08f484aaea3de6d500fa3743b4c4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a4e0123e8dca-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 65,
        "start": 65
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: No, I -- I just have one last question. What's percentage enough? There are 2.8 million transgender people in the United States. That's an awfully big figure. I do understand that in Idaho, this was the first transgender child, but that just happenstance as to location. What makes a subclass meaningful to you? Is it 1 percent, 5 percent, 30 percent, 15 percent? One is not enough for you, but why?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Mooppan",
      "case_id": "2025-24-38",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a4e0123e8dcad8f81811394cf804b00b777f08f484aaea3de6d500fa3743b4c4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a4e0123e8dca-0003-0007",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 66,
        "start": 66
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "uncertain",
      "text": "MR. MOOPPAN: With all respect, that paragraph, it says, even setting aside the physical differences, it is ludicrous to think we have to exclude infertile children from this rape law."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Mooppan",
      "case_id": "2025-24-38",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a4e0123e8dcad8f81811394cf804b00b777f08f484aaea3de6d500fa3743b4c4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a4e0123e8dca-0003-0008",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 67,
        "start": 67
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "uncertain",
      "text": "MR. MOOPPAN: I think they would have to show that, A, they are a substantial enough percentage to be able to bring an as-applied claim and then, as to that group, the law wasn't reasonably tailored for them."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2025-24-38",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a4e0123e8dcad8f81811394cf804b00b777f08f484aaea3de6d500fa3743b4c4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a4e0123e8dca-0003-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 68,
        "start": 68
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: I see. So they have to sort of get through the gate of we're big enough for you to take us seriously, but then the state has it?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2025-24-38",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a4e0123e8dcad8f81811394cf804b00b777f08f484aaea3de6d500fa3743b4c4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a4e0123e8dca-0003-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 69,
        "start": 69
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: I mean, are you really undermining the difference between the two?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2025-24-38",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a4e0123e8dcad8f81811394cf804b00b777f08f484aaea3de6d500fa3743b4c4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a4e0123e8dca-0003-0011",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 80,
        "start": 80
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "ORAL ARGUMENT OF KATHLEEN R. HARTNETT ON BEHALF OF THE RESPONDENTS"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Hartnett",
      "case_id": "2025-24-38",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a4e0123e8dcad8f81811394cf804b00b777f08f484aaea3de6d500fa3743b4c4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a4e0123e8dca-0003-0012",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 81,
        "start": 81
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "H.B. 500 thus fails heightened scrutiny as applied to Lindsay and transgender women like her who have no sex-based biological advantage as compared to birth sex females."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2025-24-38",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "a4e0123e8dcad8f81811394cf804b00b777f08f484aaea3de6d500fa3743b4c4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a4e0123e8dca-0004-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 87,
        "start": 87
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Well, that sounds an awful lot like strict scrutiny. Or, unless you're going to say whenever you can come forward with anything that is an exception to the boy/girl distinction, any case at all, you have -- you -- you can go forward with -- with a strict scrutiny challenge, whether it's -- whether it's, you know, 1 percent or whether it's 12 people, and I'm just not quite sure -- grasping why your position isn't really an effort to apply strict scrutiny to a distinction that we haven't applied it to."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Hartnett",
      "case_id": "2025-24-38",
      "confidence": 0.94,
      "document_hash": "a4e0123e8dcad8f81811394cf804b00b777f08f484aaea3de6d500fa3743b4c4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a4e0123e8dca-0004-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 92,
        "start": 92
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "uncertain",
      "text": "MS. HARTNETT: I don't think the Court has foreclosed that other than this -- this actual obvious conceptual question of, like, when do we get towards strict scrutiny? I think what I could tell you is that in the way that the Court looked at it from Caban to Lehr to Nguyen to Morales-Santana and then also Trimble and Lalli for the case of non-marital children, the Court seems to usually be trying to figure out not just is this individual somehow idiosyncratic, but are they part of a group that actually doesn't make sense to exclude?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2025-24-38",
      "confidence": 0.91,
      "document_hash": "a4e0123e8dcad8f81811394cf804b00b777f08f484aaea3de6d500fa3743b4c4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a4e0123e8dca-0004-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 95,
        "start": 95
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GORSUCH: Counsel, one might wonder whether the efforts to refashion our equal protection jurisprudence here that we've been discussing at length on sex discrimination is really a fallback from what might be -- one might wonder might have been your primary argument, which is that transgender status is itself a discreteness or a class."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "JUSTICE KAVANAUGH",
      "case_id": "2025-24-38",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a4e0123e8dcad8f81811394cf804b00b777f08f484aaea3de6d500fa3743b4c4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a4e0123e8dca-0005-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 107,
        "start": 107
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAVANAUGH: Well, just to put the big picture, and you know this table and let you respond to it, but, obviously, one of the great successes in America over the last 50 years has been the growth of women and girls' sports, and it's inspiring."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MS. HARTNETT",
      "case_id": "2025-24-38",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a4e0123e8dcad8f81811394cf804b00b777f08f484aaea3de6d500fa3743b4c4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a4e0123e8dca-0005-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 107,
        "start": 107
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "uncertain",
      "text": "We just would say that I think this is an important moment to just take a step back and say is this law actually responding to a problem in a rational manner, or is it actually overreacting on the presumption that transgender women are categorically going to be strong athletes when that's not the case."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "JUSTICE BARRETT",
      "case_id": "2025-24-38",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a4e0123e8dcad8f81811394cf804b00b777f08f484aaea3de6d500fa3743b4c4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a4e0123e8dca-0005-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 110,
        "start": 110
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BARRETT: Counsel, can I ask you a question about analytically, in the discrimination on the basis of transgender status, since trans boys can play on boys' teams, how would we say this discriminates on the basis of transgender status when its effect really only runs towards trans girls and not trans boys?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "JUSTICE GORSUCH",
      "case_id": "2025-24-38",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a4e0123e8dcad8f81811394cf804b00b777f08f484aaea3de6d500fa3743b4c4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a4e0123e8dca-0005-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 126,
        "start": 126
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GORSUCH: I appreciate all of that, but it seems to me from my glance at the record, and quite a record it is, that there is a healthy scientific dispute about the efficacy of some of these treatments, and -- and that's understandable. And I'm just wondering how does that fit with -- assume -- assume there is some dispute, and I understand the record remains to be developed further. But how does that fit with intermediate versus strict scrutiny?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2025-24-38",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "a4e0123e8dcad8f81811394cf804b00b777f08f484aaea3de6d500fa3743b4c4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a4e0123e8dca-0006-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 129,
        "start": 129
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GORSUCH: So that would remain for us to decide at a later point?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2025-24-38",
      "confidence": 0.98,
      "document_hash": "a4e0123e8dcad8f81811394cf804b00b777f08f484aaea3de6d500fa3743b4c4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a4e0123e8dca-0006-0003",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 135,
        "start": 135
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Thank you, counsel. The case is submitted."
    },
    {
